# terminal

terminal [--once] displays bytes read from mm.stdio on the selected display.
Build in a native target lane with a display and stdio provider, for example the
Linux SDL board with --target-host. A plain host build has no display provider.
Run the produced executable from the configured target output directory.

Input is polled every 10 milliseconds. The Linux console retains its existing
line discipline, so interactive input arrives when Enter is pressed. Ctrl-C
ends the tool. --once renders one read of currently available input and exits;
it is useful for smoke checks, not for draining an arbitrary stream. The console
API has no separate EOF status; zero-byte successful reads are treated as idle.
The Linux provider reports pipe hangup as an error; use file redirection for a
--once smoke check. This tool displays a stream; it does not launch a
shell, allocate a PTY, or implement ANSI control sequences.
